    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In many organisms, including humans and mice, diet-restriction is one of the most common immune responses. In mice, long-term diet restriction has been shown to decrease the age-associated reduction in immune function, effectively increasing the capacity of the immune response. Despite this, long-term diet restriction decreased mouse survival from Influenza infections. In Drosophila, anorexia can alter both host tolerance and resistance in response to microbial infection, and can be beneficial or detrimental to host survival rates, depending upon the pathogen. This suggests that diet has the ability to modulate expression of genes of the innate immune system. Although anorexia is a commonly-induced immune response, its mechanism of action is unclear. Further research into the mechanism and effects of infection-induced diet restriction on the immune system is necessary because previous research has focused mostly on long-term diet restriction. We seek to determine how infection-induced anorexia, and subsequently diet restriction, alters gene expression profiles in order to modulate an immune response.\nThe effectiveness of an immune response is shaped by the genes that are activated to fight the infection and their degree of activation at different times during the infection. Diet restricted flies that are infected with Listeria monocytogenes show decreased survival when compared to non-diet restricted flies. We hypothesized that diet-restriction could affect survival\nof infected flies by changing the dynamics of the immune response. \nRecent work from our lab has shown that many immune responsive genes, including antimicrobial peptide (AMP) genes, are expressed at levels directly dependent upon microbe number in the fly. Analyzing complete gene expression profiles for immune genes allows us to more precisely describe changes in gene induction by measuring not only maximal induction levels but the rate of gene induction. Here we show that for several AMP genes, diet restriction causes gene induction at a lower microbe concentration, thereby increasing the sensitivity of the fly to infection and reducing survival. We conclude that diet is an important factor during infection that can cause shifts in the expression of many immune genes, a fact that was\npreviously unnoticed due to the way in which gene expression was measured.",
